# Project: Robot Framework Test Automation

## Framework Structure
- Use Robot Framework as the primary keyword-driven test automation framework
- Organize test suites in .robot files under a tests/ directory
- Store custom keywords in a keywords/ directory, separated by domain
- Store reusable resources (variables, settings) in a resources/ directory
- Use a separate libraries/ directory for custom Python keyword libraries

## Coding Standards
- Follow the Robot Framework style guide: four-space indentation for separating tokens
- Use CamelCase for keyword names and UPPER_CASE for variable names
- Limit test case length to 20 steps; extract longer sequences into keywords
- Use meaningful, action-oriented keyword names that read like plain English sentences

## Test Organization
- One test suite (.robot file) per functional area or feature
- Use Suite Setup / Suite Teardown for shared preconditions and cleanup
- Use Test Setup / Test Teardown for per-test state management
- Tag tests with [Tags] for filtering (e.g., smoke, regression, critical, skip)
- Group related .robot files into subdirectories reflecting the application structure

## Best Practices
- Write tests at a high abstraction level using business-domain keywords
- Keep test cases free of implementation details; delegate details to keyword layer
- Implement the Page Object pattern via Resource files for UI automation
- Avoid hardcoded test data inside test cases; use Variables or external data files
- Use BuiltIn.Run Keyword And Continue On Failure only when explicit; prefer atomic tests

## Keyword Development
- Create three-layer keyword architecture: Business, Action, and Technical layers
- Implement Business keywords to represent user stories (e.g., "User Logs In Successfully")
- Implement Action keywords for UI interactions (e.g., "Click Submit Button")
- Implement Technical keywords that wrap library calls with error handling
- Store keywords in separate .resource files; import via Resource in test suites

## Variable Management
- Define environment-specific variables in separate variable files (e.g., dev.yaml, prod.yaml)
- Pass variable files via --variablefile at execution time for environment switching
- Use ${VAR} for scalar, @{LIST} for list, and &{DICT} for dictionary variables
- Store secrets in environment variables; never hardcode credentials in .robot files
- Use VAR syntax (RF 7+) for inline variable assignment within keywords

## Library Usage
- Use SeleniumLibrary for web UI testing with explicit locator strategies (id, css, xpath)
- Use RequestsLibrary for REST API testing
- Use DatabaseLibrary for database validation in integration tests
- Implement custom Python libraries by extending robot.api.deco decorators
- Pin library versions in requirements.txt for reproducible execution

## Locator Strategy
- Prefer data-testid or data-qa attributes as primary locators for web elements
- Use CSS selectors over XPath for performance and readability
- Store locators as variables in page-specific Resource files
- Use SeleniumLibrary's Page Should Contain Element with custom error messages

## Execution and Configuration
- Use robot.yaml or a Makefile to define standard execution commands
- Configure output directory (--outputdir results/) and log level (--loglevel INFO)
- Use --include and --exclude tags to control which tests run in each environment
- Enable parallel execution with pabot (Parallel Robot Framework executor) for large suites

## Reporting
- Use the built-in log.html and report.html for local review after each run
- Integrate with Allure via robotframework-allure-listener for richer CI reports
- Archive output.xml artifacts in CI for post-run result analysis
- Configure --xunit for JUnit-compatible XML output in CI/CD pipelines

## CI/CD Integration
- Run smoke tests on every pull request; run full regression on merge to main
- Use Docker with a pre-built Robot Framework image for consistent CI environments
- Configure failure thresholds with --noncritical tags to distinguish advisory failures
- Upload log.html and report.html as CI artifacts for post-run debugging

## Test Data Management
- Store static test data in YAML or JSON files under a testdata/ directory
- Load test data using the Collections and yaml libraries
- Implement data-driven tests with the [Template] syntax and YAML data files
- Clean up test data in Suite Teardown to leave the environment in a known state

## Documentation
- Add [Documentation] to every test case describing its purpose and scope
- Add [Documentation] to every keyword explaining its parameters and expected behavior
- Maintain a README in each test suite directory describing setup and execution steps
- Use --doc flag output to auto-generate keyword documentation with libdoc

Remember to keep Robot Framework tests readable by non-technical stakeholders — clarity and plain-English keyword names are the framework's primary strength.
